RISC3D
======
Configure's RISC3D window shows what RISC3D, RISC OS's 3D driver interface,
is doing:

  Driver           the 3D driver RISC3D loaded from its drivers folder
                   (!Boot.Resources.!RISC3D.GPU): Linux3D on RISC OS on
                   Linux, V3DRender on a Raspberry Pi 4, 400 or CM4
  Draws            on the GPU, or in software
  Renderer         the GPU and driver doing the drawing
  OpenGL,
  OpenGL ES        the newest versions programs can have
  Largest texture,
  Extensions       what the GPU offers
  Shared contexts  whether a program's contexts can share textures and
                   buffers (web browsers draw this way)
  Contexts open    3D contexts programs have open now
  Drivers          the drivers folder, and what draws now: the driver
                   in use (Linux3D, V3DRender, ...), or Software3D when
                   none is (Mesa drawing in software in each program)

On RISC OS on Linux it opens an OpenGL, an OpenGL ES and an OpenGL ES 1.1
context for a moment to ask. With Rob Heaton's V3D driver, a Pi's
V3DRender doesn't start (the two can't share the GPU yet).

Load a 3D driver (the one tick box), unticked and Set, turns RISC3D's GPU drawing off: no
driver is loaded, and programs draw in software as they did before RISC3D.
The choice is kept in Choices:RISC3D.Settings (*Set RISC3D$Off 1), which
!RISC3D runs at start-up. It takes effect at once when no program has a 3D
context open, else at the next start.

Set leaves the window open, showing the result. Refresh looks again;
Close closes the window. *RISC3D at the command line gives the same.

